]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Examples of Heuristics]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[1. Availability heuristic 📚]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[2. Representativeness heuristic 🖼️]
#v(20pt)
#only("3-")[3. Anchoring and adjustment heuristic ⚓🔧]
#only("1")[
#voiceover("One common heuristic is the availability heuristic, where people judge the likelihood of an event based on how easily examples come to mind.")
]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("Another is the representativeness heuristic, where people categorize situations based on their similarity to stereotypes, ignoring base rates.")
]
#only("3")[
#voiceover("The anchoring and adjustment heuristic involves making estimates by starting from an initial value and adjusting insufficiently, leading to estimates biased towards the anchor.")
]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Impact on Financial Decisions]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[- Overreliance on recent or salient information 📰🔍]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[- Stereotyping investments based on superficial characteristics 🏷️🎭]
#v(20pt)
#only("3-")[- Insufficient adjustment from arbitrary reference points 📊📈]
#only("1")[
#voiceover("In financial decision-making, the availability heuristic can lead investors to overrely on recent or salient information, such as media coverage or personal experiences.")
]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("The representativeness heuristic can cause investors to stereotype investments based on superficial characteristics, ignoring fundamentals.")
]
#only("3")[
#voiceover("Anchoring and adjustment can result in investors making insufficient adjustments from arbitrary reference points, such as purchase prices or market indices.")
]
]

